 ],
 "literal": "calf its yielding breast to drink, in the front yard / millet female elephant eating great mountain land-lord! / ruined place-at rejoiced help throne / grandeur having obtained forgotten king like / gratitude having forgotten you will not abide if, soft grace / proud peacock's plumage like her / thick soft hair rightful indeed to you!",
 "translation": "O lord of the land of great mountains, where a female elephant feeds on millet in the front yard while her calf drinks from her yielding udders! If you do not live forgetting the gratitude, unlike a king who forgot the help gladly given to him when he was in a ruined state once he regained his grand throne, then her thick, soft hair, beautiful like the plumage of a proud peacock, is indeed rightfully yours.",
 "modern": "தன் கன்று பால் சுரக்கும் மடியை உண்ண, முற்றத்தில் தினையை பெண் யானை உண்ணும் பெரிய மலைநாட்டிற்குத் தலைவனே! தான் அழிந்த காலத்தில் தனக்கு மகிழ்ச்சியோடு செய்த உதவியை, அரியணையைப் பெற்றுச் சிறப்புற்ற பின் மறந்துவிட்ட அரசனைப் போல, நீ செய்த நன்றியை மறந்து வாழமாட்டாய் என்றால், செருக்குற்ற மயிலின் தோகையைப் போன்ற இவளுடைய அடர்ந்த மென்மையான கூந்தல் உனக்கே உரியதாகும்.",
 "explanation": "The friend (தோழி) speaks to the hero (தலைவன்) who has been delaying his marriage to the heroine. She describes his mountain land (Kuriñci) where a female elephant eats millet in the front yard while her calf drinks her milk, an implied metaphor (ullurai) suggesting that the hero should openly protect and cherish the heroine. She compares a man who might abandon his love to an ungrateful king who, after regaining his grand throne, forgets those who helped him in his days of ruin. She asserts that if the hero is truly grateful and faithful, the heroine's beautiful, thick, soft hair—which resembles the plumage of a proud peacock—rightfully belongs to him through marriage. This landscape imagery emphasizes the necessity of marital commitment and the virtue of gratitude.",
